          %h2#Idea Idea
          %h3#IdeaGeneral General
          %p
            %em Persistent cohomotopy
            (not yet an established term) would be the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomotopy"} cohomotopy
            in situations where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} space
            and/or its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} map
            to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"} n-sphere
            may depend on a parameter, to yield not just a single
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomotopy+set"} cohomotopy set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+object"} filtered
            set (a filtered
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            when the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+cell+complex"} dimension
            of the domain space is large enough). One concrete implementation of this general notion is considered in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FranekKrcal17"} Franek &amp; Krčál 2017
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Definition"} below
          %p
            Generally, one may understand persistent cohomotopy both as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality"} dual
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homotopy"} persistent homotopy
            as well as a
            = succeed "-version" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology"} persistent homology
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                %th coarse
                %th intermediate
                %th fine
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+homology"} generalized homology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology"} generalized cohomology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomotopy"} cohomotopy
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong persistent homology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology"} persistent ordinary homology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} persistent generalized homology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homotopy"} persistent homotopy
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %strong persistent cohomology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} persistent ordinary cohomology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} persistent generalized cohomology
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+cohomotopy"} persistent cohomotopy
          %h3#DetectingDataMeetingTargets Detecting data meeting targets
          %p
            While
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology"} persistent homology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+data+analysis"} topological data analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+diagram"} persistent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycles"} cycles
            in a given data set, it is typically unclear what a persistent cycle actually
            %em means
            for the practical interpretation of the data – certainly this information is not provided by the mathematics.
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 0px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/IndicatorFunctionOnData-220523.jpg", :width => "420px"}/
          %p
            In contrast,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+cohomotopy"} persistent cohomotopy
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TDA"} TDA
            is the effective answer to a concrete and common question in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data+analysis"} data analysis
          %p
            Given a large-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+CW-complex"} dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} space
            of data, and a small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"}> real
            ) indicator values assigned to each data point with given precision
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/r
            %em
              does
              %em any
              data meet a prescribed target indication precisely?
          %p
            A fundamental theorem of persistent Cohomotopy (
            %a{:href => "persistent+cohomotopy#FranekKrcalWagner18"}> Franek, Krčál &amp; Wagner 2018
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "persistent+cohomotopy#FranekKrcal17"} Franek &amp; Krčál 2017, p. 5
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GuaranteeThatSolutionsExist"}
            below) shows that (1.) the answer to this question is detected by a certain
            = succeed "-class" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy"} Cohomotopy
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computability"} computable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidability"} decidable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mphantom
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phantom{----}
            (figure from
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/New+Foundations+for+TDA+--+Cohomotopy"} SS22
          %p
            Alternatively, with tools from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology+theory"} persistent homology theory
            an answer to this question is given by the method of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well+groups"} well groups
            – but (1.) it is known that well groups are in general too coarse to provide a complete answer and (2.) despite effort it remains unknown if well groups are actually
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computability"} computable
            in relevant cases, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "persistent+cohomotopy#FranekKrcal16"} Franek &amp; Krčál 2016
          %p
            In this concrete sense (and generally by the
            %a{:href => "#IdeaGeneral"} above
            discussion), persistent cohomotopy may be understood as an enhancement or refinement of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well+groups"} well groups
            in)
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology"} persistent homology
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/EnhancePersFromHolomogyToCohomotopy-220522.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              (graphics from
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/New+Foundations+for+TDA+--+Cohomotopy"} SS 22
          %h3#UseOfCobordismTheory Use of cobordism theory
          %p
            If the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of data may be assumed to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            (indeed, in typical examples
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is itself a large-dimensional
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontryagin%27s+theorem"} Pontryagin's theorem
            as characterizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/iso-hypersurfaces"} iso-hypersurfaces
            of data (close to a given target indicator) by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+cobordism"} framed
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+theory"} cobordism theory
            (
            %a{:href => "persistent+cohomotopy#FranekKrcal17"}> Franek &amp; Krčál 2017, p. 8-9
            ). The full implications of this relation for topological data analysis remain to be explored.
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p
            We spell out the definition considered in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FranekKrcal17"} Franek &amp; Krčál 2017
          %p For
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
                a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mo &gt;
                          %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in \mathbb{R}_{\gt 0}
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \,\colon\, X \xrightarrow{\;\;\;\;\;\;} \mathbb{R}^n
                a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
          %p such that with
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi r
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                      %mo ∖
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mi r
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mover
                        %mo ↪
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi i
                            %mi r
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi X
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_r \,\coloneqq\, f^{-1}\big( \mathbb{R}^n \setminus B_r \big) \;\xhookrightarrow{i_r}\; X\;
              %p
                (the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
                under
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius"} radius
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
                around the origin in the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
          %p we have that
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,A_r)
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-pair"} CW-pair
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+cell+complex"} dimension
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi dim
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim(X) \leq 2n - 3
          %p
            consider the following induced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exact sequence
            of
            = succeed "-groups" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomotopy"} cohomotopy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,Y]
            denote the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
