          %p
          #GuaranteeThatSolutionsExist.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a{:href => "#FranekKrcal17"}> Franek &amp; Krčál 2017
                , following
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#FranekKrcal16"} 2016, p. 5
              %br/
              Under the above assumptions, the following are equivalent:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  there exists an
                  = succeed "-deformation" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi r
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
                  %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:rDeformation"} (4)
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  with
                  %em no
                  zeros on
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo ∖
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mi r
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \setminus A_r
              %li
                %p
                  the Cohomotopy class
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi f
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mi r
                        %mo ∈
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mi n
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo ,
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [f]_r \in \pi_n^{(f,r)}(X)
                  %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:FunctionAsClassInThePersistenceCohomotopyRelativeToIt"} (2)
                  is trivial.
            %p
              Moreover, both questions are effectively
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computability"} computable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidability"} decidable
              in the given dimension range.
          %p
            The proof of the computability statement in Thm.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GuaranteeThatSolutionsExist"}
            uses a general result about computability of
            = succeed "-sets" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomotopy"} cohomotopy
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "computational+topology#CKMSVW14"} CKMSVW14
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
            Consider the simplest instructive example, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
